    {
      "id": "1354",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1354,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "In Japanese, the dictionary form and the non-past form of verbs are identical. For example, 食べる is both the dictionary form and the non-past form of the verb 'to eat.' Unlike in English, this form can be used as is to express future actions ('I will eat'), habitual actions ('I regularly eat / I eat everyday'), or general truths ('Lions eat meat').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 950,
          "slug": "verb-non-past",
          "title": "Verbs (Non-past)",
          "meaning": "(Non-past)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 12,
          "slug": "る-Verbs",
          "title": "る-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "る + ます (Ichidan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1335",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 1335,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The dictionary form of a る-verb is its base form, expressing current, habitual, or future actions (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The past form (-た form) describes completed actions or past states (e.g., 食べた = 'I ate'). Both forms are commonly used in casual speech and various grammar patterns requiring plain verb forms, such as ～と思います ('I think').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 12,
          "slug": "る-Verbs",
          "title": "る-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "る + ます (Ichidan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 895,
          "slug": "る-verb-past",
          "title": "る-Verb (Past)",
          "meaning": "る - Verbs (Past tense)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "45",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 45,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "る-Verbs and う-Verbs are the two main verb types in Japanese. Their dictionary forms are the basic forms used in dictionaries and casual non-past sentences (e.g., 食べる = 'I eat' or 'I will eat'). The difference between these verb types is in their conjugation patterns, not their meaning or nuance. Be careful: not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs (for example, 帰る 'to return' is actually an う-Verb).",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 12,
          "slug": "る-Verbs",
          "title": "る-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "る + ます (Ichidan Verbs)",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 26,
          "slug": "う-Verbs",
          "title": "う-Verb (Dictionary)",
          "meaning": "う + ます (Godan Verbs)",
