        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "The dictionary form of verbs is their base form, expressing habitual or future actions. The past form indicates completed actions or past states. The difference between る-Verbs and う-Verbs is how they conjugate into the past, not in their meaning. For example, る-Verbs change る to た (食べる → 食べた), while う-Verbs follow different patterns (読む → 読んだ). Be aware that not all verbs ending in る are る-Verbs—帰る ('to return') is an う-Verb.",
